{% comment %}
  Scoped styling for the desktop mega menu (the subjects menu, 8 L1 / 31 L2 / 126 L3).

  Rendered from snippets/gazelle-layout.liquid, NOT from sections/header.liquid —
  header.liquid is a Horizon core file and must not be edited. gazelle-layout is
  already render-hooked in theme.liquid, so this adds no new core-file touch.

  ⚠️ MOST OF THE MEGA MENU IS NATIVE. Read this before adding anything here.
  blocks/_header-menu.liquid drives BOTH levels entirely from CSS variables
  (--menu-parent-font-* / --menu-child-font-*), derived in
  snippets/submenu-font-styles.liquid from three block settings:

    type_font_primary_link  font family/weight/style for BOTH levels (shared)
    type_case_primary_link  uppercase or none, for BOTH levels (shared)
    menu_font_style         the size + colour PRESET pair, three options:

      regular        parent = md size, full foreground   | child = sm size, subdued
      inverse        parent = sm size, SUBDUED           | child = md size, full   <- Horizon default
      inverse_large  parent = sm size, subdued           | child = xl size, full

  "Column headings heavier than their links" is therefore a SETTING, not CSS:
  menu_font_style must be 'regular'. The default 'inverse' does the exact
  opposite — headings smaller and greyed, links larger and darker.

  What genuinely is NOT available natively, and so lives here:
    1. font-weight differing between parent and child. Both read the same
       --font-<preset>--weight token, so no setting can separate them.
    2. a distinct treatment for the trailing "All <parent>" link.
    3. the panel's own background / border.

  The "All X" hook: those items carry no distinguishing class — mega-menu-list.liquid
  emits every third-level link as a plain .mega-menu__link. But build_gazelle_menu.py
  always appends "All <parent>" as the LAST item of each column's list, so
  li:last-child is a reliable structural hook and NO label change is needed.
  (The brief's fallback of prefixing labels with "→ " was not used — a CSS hook
  beats editing 20 menu labels.)

  ⚠️ .mega-menu__link--parent is applied only when a link HAS children
  (mega-menu-list.liquid:112). The 4 merch items under "Gifts & stationery" and the
  7 links under "Trade & publishers" have none, so they render as plain
  .mega-menu__link and will NOT pick up the heading treatment. That is arguably
  correct — they are links, not column headings — but those two panels will read
  differently from the six subject panels.
{% endcomment %}

{% style %}
  /* 1. Column headings — weight only. Size and colour come from
     menu_font_style: regular; do not restate them here or the setting stops
     being the control. */
  .mega-menu__link--parent {
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  /* 2. Third-level links. ~15px at a 1.35 line height keeps a 9-item column
     on one screen at 13", which the native sm token does not guarantee. */
  .mega-menu__list .mega-menu__link:not(.mega-menu__link--parent) {
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    line-height: 1.35;
  }

  /* 3. The trailing "All <parent>" link — last item of each column's child list.
     Muted and letterspaced so it reads as a way out of the column rather than
     another sibling subject. */
  .mega-menu__list ul > li:last-child > .mega-menu__link {
    color: rgb(var(--color-foreground-rgb) / var(--opacity-subdued-text));
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
  }

  .mega-menu__list ul > li:last-child > .mega-menu__link:hover {
    color: var(--color-foreground);
  }

  /* 4. Panel width. The panel div carries `section section--full-width-margin
     section--page-width`, so core's `.section--page-width > * { grid-column: 2 }`
     should already drop the grid into the centred column — but it renders
     full-bleed with content on the left edge, and the header's Width = Page
     setting (section_width: 'page-width', confirmed in header-group.json) does
     not constrain it. Rather than fight whatever resolves --page-width inside
     the header's stacking context, pin the grid to the SAME token the rest of
     the site uses, so the menu shares the 1200px rail with everything else. */
  .mega-menu .mega-menu__grid {
    width: 100%;
    max-width: var(--page-content-width);
    margin-inline: auto;
  }

  /* 5. Column sizing.
     mega-menu-list.liquid hardcodes `--menu-columns-desktop: 6` for every text
     panel (grid_columns_count, blocks/_header-menu.liquid:122), so the grid is
     always six equal tracks no matter how many columns a panel holds. At six
     tracks the columns are too narrow for headings like "Children's & Young
     Adult", and a one-column panel is left with five empty tracks.

     auto-fit sizes tracks to the content: empty tracks collapse, and their
     gutters with them.

     The max is 1fr, NOT a fixed px ceiling. An earlier version used
     minmax(200px, 224px) on the assumption that the grid is 1200px wide, so
     five tracks plus gaps would land exactly on 1200. That assumption is
     wrong in practice — page padding and the editor preview make the
     container narrower — and the result was the fifth column crushing to a
     few characters instead of wrapping. With 1fr the tracks divide whatever
     width is actually available, and 200px is a hard floor: if five will not
     fit at 200px, the fifth WRAPS to a second row rather than being crushed.

     The 1200px cap belongs on the grid (rule 4 above), not on the sum of the
     tracks. */
  @media screen and (min-width: 990px) {
    .mega-menu .mega-menu__grid {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(200px, 1fr));
    }

    /* A lone column would otherwise take the whole 1fr track and run the full
       width of the grid. Cap it so Children's reads as a column sitting on the
       left rail, at roughly the width it has in a multi-column panel.
       :only-child is exact — it cannot affect any panel that has more than one
       column. */
    .mega-menu .mega-menu__grid > .mega-menu__column:only-child {
      max-width: 260px;
    }
  }

  /* 6. The panel sheet. Off-white with a hairline top edge and no shadow, per
     the Waterstones reference (a calm sheet, not a floating card).
     #f3efe8 is the theme's existing cover-panel token, kept deliberately so the
     menu and the product hero share one off-white. */
  .mega-menu {
    background-color: #f3efe8;
    border-block-start: 1px solid var(--color-border, #e8e2d8);
  }
{% endstyle %}
